{"name":"Toy Mauve","hex":"#776ea2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"toy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Topoiyo."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something to play with, especially as intended for use by a child. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A thing of little importance or value; a trifle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toy"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Toy","blocks":[{"text":"A toy or plaything is an object that is used primarily to provide entertainment. Simple examples include toy blocks, board games, and dolls. Toys are often designed for use by children, although many are designed specifically for adults and pets. Toys can provide utilitarian benefits, including physical exercise, cultural awareness, or academic education. Additionally, utilitarian objects, especially those which are no longer needed for their original purpose, can be used as toys. Examples include children building a fort with empty cereal boxes and tissue paper spools, or a toddler playing with a broken TV remote. The term \"toy\" can also be used to refer to utilitarian objects purchased for enjoyment rather than need, or for expensive necessities for which a large fraction of the cost represents its ability to provide enjoyment to the owner, such as luxury cars, high-end motorcycles, gaming computers, and flagship smartphones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Toy_car_%28AM_1996.165.95%29.jpg/330px-Toy_car_%28AM_1996.165.95%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toy"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg/500px-Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg/1280px-Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg/500px-Little_horse_on_wheels_%28Ancient_greek_child%27s_Toy%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Little horse on wheels, Ancient Greek children's toy. From a tomb dating 950–900 BCE, Kerameikos Archaeological Museum, Athens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALittle_horse_on_wheels_(Ancient_greek_child's_Toy).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Reif_Spielzeug.jpg/250px-Reif_Spielzeug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Reif_Spielzeug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn